.TH CRONTAB 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
crontab \- schedule periodic commands
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B crontab
.RI [ file ]
.br
.B crontab
.B \-e
.br
.B crontab
.B \-l
.br
.B crontab
.B \-r
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B crontab
installs, edits, lists, or removes the invoking user's crontab, a file
of scheduled commands that
.BR crond (1)
runs when it is running. There being exactly one real user identity on
this system, there is exactly one crontab; there is no way to name, or
operate on, any other user's crontab, and no
.B \-u
option.
.PP
With a
.I file
operand, the current crontab is replaced with the contents of
.IR file .
With no operand at all, the new crontab is read from standard input.
In both cases, the input is fully validated (see below) before being
installed; on a validation failure, nothing is installed and the
previous crontab, if any, is left untouched.
.PP
.B \-l
writes the current crontab to standard output, unmodified.
.PP
.B \-r
removes the current crontab entirely.
.PP
.B \-e
edits the current crontab interactively: its contents (or an empty
file, if none exists yet) are copied to a temporary file, which is
then opened in
.BR $VISUAL ,
or
.B $EDITOR
if
.B $VISUAL
is unset, or
.BR ed (1)
if neither is set. If the editor exits with status 0, the edited file
is validated and, if valid, installed as the new crontab. If the
editor exits with a nonzero status, or the edited file fails
validation, nothing is installed, and the path of the edited temporary
file is printed so its contents can be recovered; unlike some
implementations,
.B \-e
does not automatically loop back into the editor on a validation
failure \(em re\-running
.B "crontab \-e"
is required.
.SH INPUT VALIDATION
Every line of the input is required to be blank, a comment (beginning
with
.BR # ) ,
or five whitespace\-separated schedule fields \(em minute, hour, day of
month, month, and day of week, in that order \(em followed by a command
field. This is checked line by line, eagerly, at install time, not
deferred until
.B crond
happens to reach a given line.
.PP
A line that looks like an environment\-variable assignment (an
identifier followed by
.BR = ,
e.g.
.RB \` MAILTO=user \' )
is refused with its own specific diagnostic, distinguishing it from an
ordinary malformed line: this is real crontab(5) syntax, but there is
no mail subsystem here for
.B MAILTO
or any other assignment to affect, so accepting the syntax while doing
nothing with it is refused rather than silently misrepresented.
.PP
Each schedule field accepts:
.RS
.IP \(bu 3
an asterisk, matching every value in that field's range;
.IP \(bu 3
a single number;
.IP \(bu 3
an inclusive range,
.IR low \- high ;
.IP \(bu 3
a step, written as
.I field
.B /
.I N
(every Nth value, starting at the field's own minimum) where
.I field
is an asterisk or a range;
.IP \(bu 3
a comma\-separated list of any of the above.
.RE
.PP
The month field additionally accepts the names
.BR jan " through " dec ,
and the day\-of\-week field the names
.BR sun " through " sat ,
matched case\-insensitively against their first three letters, in
place of a number anywhere a number is accepted. Field ranges are:
minute 0\-59, hour 0\-23, day of month 1\-31, month 1\-12, and day of
week 0\-7 (both 0 and 7 mean Sunday).
.PP
If day of month is given as a literal asterisk but day of week is
not, the two fields are combined with OR rather than AND: the command
runs on every day matching the day\-of\-week field, regardless of the
day of month. In every other combination, a day matches only when
both the day\-of\-month and day\-of\-week fields match it.
.PP
Not implemented:
.RB \` @reboot \' / \` @daily \'\-style
nickname shorthands for a fixed schedule pattern.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I file
A pathname to install as the new crontab, replacing the current one.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The requested operation completed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred: a syntax error in the crontab being installed (the
offending line number is reported), a missing crontab to list or
remove,
.I file
could not be opened, the editor could not be found or run, or the
crontab spool could not be accessed.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR crond (1),
.BR at (1),
.BR atd (1),
.BR batch (1),
.BR ed (1)
